<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pantheon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 126</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Pantheum, Basilica of Saint Mary and the Holy Martyrs, Santa Maria Rotonda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pantheon is an ancient Roman temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, built on the site of an earlier temple commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa</a> under Augustus and rebuilt by the emperor Hadrian, probably dedicated around 126 CE. Its exact date is uncertain because Hadrian re-inscribed it with Agrippa's original inscription. A portico of granite Corinthian columns leads to a rotunda under a coffered concrete dome with an oculus; the height to the oculus and the interior diameter are both 43 metres, and it is still the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world. Since 609 it has been a church, which helped to preserve it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pantheon-rome.jpg" alt="Pantheon" width="330" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NikonZ7II · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pantheon_(Rome)_-_Right_side_and_front.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
